      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Two Halves, One Frame: Testing Spatial Repetition in Syndromes and a Century",
        "central_argument": "A filmmaker can test whether framing, camera position, and spatial repetition distinguish the film's two halves by treating the critic's reported contrast—warm, intimate first half versus cold, detached second half—as a hypothesis to be verified through systematic logging of shot scale, camera angle, offscreen space, and recurring locations. Because no clip evidence is supplied, every formal claim must remain conditional: the task is not to assert what the film does, but to design observable tests that would confirm or refute the critic's interpretation. The central question is whether the two halves use the same spatial grammar with different emotional inflections, or whether they employ fundamentally different framing and staging strategies. By comparing repeated scenes—such as the dentist and monk consultations mentioned by the critic—and by tracking how the camera frames bodies, architecture, and empty spaces, the filmmaker can distinguish between a unified style and a deliberate bifurcation.",
        "section_lenses": [
          "Framing and shot scale as emotional temperature",
          "Camera position and the vector line in repeated encounters",
          "Offscreen space and the implied world",
          "Spatial repetition and the recurring square fitness exercise",
          "Deep space, lens choice, and the architecture of the two worlds"
        ],
